1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It transports electricity, water, and heat.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Our bodies can't survive without it.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Yet it can kill microbes in minutes.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It brings music to our ears

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
and beauty to our eyes.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
It ended the Stone Age

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
and ushered in the Information Age.

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
This versatile red metal has been indispensable to our technological success.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Copper on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:50,940
San Diego, California.

11
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,060
NASCO, the National Steel and Shipbuilding Company,

12
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:58,460
has delivered over 110 ships to the world's fleets

13
00:00:58,461 --> 00:00:59,780
during the last four decades.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,520
It's here where all manner of huge ships take shape, from oil tankers to container ships.

15
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:18,100
And they all require copper, hundreds of tons of copper.

16
00:01:18,101 --> 00:01:23,120
Here close to 1,200 workers are busy assembling one of 11 identical cargo ships for the U.S.

17
00:01:23,121 --> 00:01:26,340
Navy.

18
00:01:26,341 --> 00:01:31,300
The first of the ships, the USNS Lewis and Clark, is already in service.

19
00:01:31,301 --> 00:01:38,800
This ship is the newest and largest auxiliary ship in the US Navy.

20
00:01:38,801 --> 00:01:44,160
Our length is 688 feet and our width is 106 feet.

21
00:01:44,161 --> 00:01:50,480
Each of these electrically powered ships requires an extraordinary amount of wiring to operate.

22
00:01:50,481 --> 00:01:56,180
There's approximately 500 miles of copper cable on board this ship that is used to

23
00:01:56,181 --> 00:01:59,720
to distribute electricity for the low voltage systems,

24
00:01:59,721 --> 00:02:03,680
and then our high voltage systems that run at 6,600 volts,

25
00:02:03,681 --> 00:02:05,520
which is used for the propulsion system

26
00:02:05,521 --> 00:02:08,820
to drive the ship through the water.

27
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:10,500
But copper is used for much more

28
00:02:10,501 --> 00:02:13,200
than just the wiring of these vessels.

29
00:02:13,201 --> 00:02:16,300
We utilize copper in the paint on the bottom of the ship

30
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:18,200
to prevent marine fouling.

31
00:02:18,201 --> 00:02:21,240
We use copper inside the ship in alloys

32
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:24,500
to pipe seawater systems and prevent corrosion

33
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:26,200
in those seawater systems.

34
00:02:26,201 --> 00:02:29,360
And we use copper also in the propeller as an alloy

35
00:02:29,361 --> 00:02:32,400
to ensure a long service life for the propeller.

36
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:34,740
Copper is the metal of choice for any part of the ship

37
00:02:34,741 --> 00:02:38,120
that comes in contact with the sea

38
00:02:38,121 --> 00:02:41,700
because of one of copper's most valuable properties,

39
00:02:41,701 --> 00:02:45,580
its resistance to saltwater corrosion.

40
00:02:45,581 --> 00:02:48,140
Immediately upon contact with water,

41
00:02:48,141 --> 00:02:50,340
the metal develops a protective oxide coating.

42
00:02:50,341 --> 00:02:58,960
Merchant vessels and warships demand durability, and copper stands the test of time in one

43
00:02:58,961 --> 00:03:04,080
of the most corrosive environments on Earth.

44
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:10,580
To make pumps, valves, and onboard desalination systems, the shipbuilders use a special copper

45
00:03:10,581 --> 00:03:13,220
and nickel alloy called cupronickel.

46
00:03:13,221 --> 00:03:20,260
In order to prevent the seawater systems from corroding, we have constructed them out

47
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:24,040
of copper nickel, and that copper is very resistant

48
00:03:24,041 --> 00:03:25,160
to corrosion.

49
00:03:25,161 --> 00:03:28,560
When sea water is piped through a copper nickel pipe,

50
00:03:28,561 --> 00:03:31,880
you actually get a layer forms on the inside of the pipe

51
00:03:31,881 --> 00:03:37,300
of oxides that then stop the corrosion process.

52
00:03:37,301 --> 00:03:42,820
The alloy is generally 70% copper and 30% nickel.

53
00:03:42,821 --> 00:03:45,460
The addition of nickel increases both copper's strength

54
00:03:45,461 --> 00:03:46,760
and corrosion resistance.

55
00:03:46,761 --> 00:03:54,480
Copper is also alloyed with harder metals to make one of the strongest and largest parts

56
00:03:54,481 --> 00:04:04,620
of each ship, a 22-foot-wide, 40-ton corrosion-resistant propeller.

57
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:11,160
The naval architects who fabricate such massive propellers use a centuries-old casting technique

58
00:04:11,161 --> 00:04:14,160
combined with some new manufacturing technologies.

59
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:19,160
Propellers are cast in one pore generally,

60
00:04:19,161 --> 00:04:23,160
and then when the casting is completed, the mold is taken off,

61
00:04:23,161 --> 00:04:27,160
and then the cast propeller would be cleaned, sanded,

62
00:04:27,161 --> 00:04:31,160
and then shaped rather than having to build a model of a ship.

63
00:04:31,161 --> 00:04:36,160
They can do that with a 3D model now and generally get a fair idea

64
00:04:36,161 --> 00:04:40,160
as to how the impact of the design is going to work against the hull.

65
00:04:40,161 --> 00:04:44,980
Once the propeller is shaped and cast, then it's machined to a very fine tolerance.

66
00:04:45,540 --> 00:04:51,140
They have to be able to shape the propeller blade profiles to an exact shape

67
00:04:51,141 --> 00:04:55,720
to ensure long service life, prevent vibration, and also prevent noise,

68
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:57,820
which can create problems for the vessel.

69
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:03,040
Although copper alloy propellers can withstand years of seawater exposure,

70
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:06,040
they can still be damaged by ocean debris.

71
00:05:06,041 --> 00:05:13,040
engineers routinely inspect used propellers with staining chemicals and magnifying glasses

72
00:05:13,041 --> 00:05:15,380
to find even the tiniest flaws

73
00:05:15,381 --> 00:05:24,300
a small crack can promulgate out and you can have a catastrophic loss of blade section based on the

74
00:05:24,301 --> 00:05:27,380
small crack being allowed to not be repaired and grow

75
00:05:27,381 --> 00:05:31,320
we do a visual inspection and then based on that visual inspection we

76
00:05:31,321 --> 00:05:34,880
will determine what the customer needs for the repair.

77
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:39,040
Generally, it'll be welding, grinding, balancing the propeller.

78
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:44,340
Copper also protects vessels from tiny but dangerous sea creatures

79
00:05:44,341 --> 00:05:47,460
that, given enough time, could even sink a ship.

80
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:51,380
Copper is antimicrobial,

81
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:54,160
meaning microorganisms can't survive on it.

82
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:59,380
Scientists theorize that copper kills microbes by damaging their DNA.

83
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:04,320
It's been used to protect ships from marine organisms

84
00:06:04,321 --> 00:06:08,320
since the 18th century, when the British Royal Navy

85
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:10,320
first clad the hulls of their wooden warships

86
00:06:10,321 --> 00:06:13,320
with copper sheet.

87
00:06:13,321 --> 00:06:16,320
Copper protects timbers from sea worms

88
00:06:16,321 --> 00:06:19,320
that can bore through hulls.

89
00:06:19,321 --> 00:06:22,320
It also repels barnacles, which create drag.

90
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:26,320
Nowadays, with the steel hulls, we still need something

91
00:06:26,321 --> 00:06:29,320
to prevent sea life from adhering to the hull,

92
00:06:29,321 --> 00:06:32,960
And they use a copper paint or a paint with copper in it.

93
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:37,940
And the copper basically prevents any of the sea life from wanting to grow on the side of the ship.

94
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:45,040
Through the centuries, shipbuilders have continually taken advantage of copper's benefits at sea.

95
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:54,120
One of America's most notable ships, the USS Constitution, often referred to as old iron sides,

96
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:57,760
was actually clad in copper, not iron.

97
00:06:57,761 --> 00:07:05,040
When the ship was built in Edmund Hart's shipyard in 1794 to 1798, they sheathed the

98
00:07:05,041 --> 00:07:07,240
bottom of the hull of the ship with copper.

99
00:07:07,241 --> 00:07:11,840
This original copper, when she was built, was supplied by Great Britain.

100
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:13,680
They actually imported it.

101
00:07:13,681 --> 00:07:18,040
Paul Revere actually supplied the original copper bolts and fasteners on board the ship

102
00:07:18,041 --> 00:07:20,040
during construction.

103
00:07:20,041 --> 00:07:26,480
In 1803, when the Sea Worn vessel came to port for repairs, Paul Revere decided it was

104
00:07:26,481 --> 00:07:30,980
time to make copper sheeting in America.

105
00:07:30,981 --> 00:07:36,980
When the Constitution was refurbished in 1803, Paul Revere,

106
00:07:36,981 --> 00:07:39,980
thanks to help from the government, plus his own money,

107
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:43,480
developed a rolling process in Canton, Massachusetts,

108
00:07:43,481 --> 00:07:45,080
and rolled the copper.

109
00:07:45,081 --> 00:07:49,480
So the second sheathing of the Constitution was done by Revere

110
00:07:49,481 --> 00:07:54,880
metal, with Revere metal, made in this country.

111
00:07:54,881 --> 00:07:58,120
The hull isn't the only place on the ship where copper sheeting was put to use.

112
00:07:59,660 --> 00:08:02,940
Another small but critical area was completely lined with copper.

113
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:08,100
This is where young boys on board the ship would come down during battle

114
00:08:08,101 --> 00:08:11,560
to retrieve about six pounds of gunpowder, which would be in this powder magazine,

115
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:13,100
which is lined with copper.

116
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:17,700
Copper is a non-ferrous metal. It wouldn't spark as easily as you can imagine

117
00:08:17,701 --> 00:08:18,680
with all the gunpowder.

118
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:22,900
Down below, if there was a spark, it could ignite the whole ship

119
00:08:22,901 --> 00:08:24,440
and you could actually lose your ship.

120
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:28,880
Though copper's merits at sea have been known for centuries,

121
00:08:28,881 --> 00:08:31,880
we've been taking advantage of the red metal's array of

122
00:08:31,881 --> 00:08:34,880
attributes for close to 10,000 years.

123
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:41,880
In that time, copper has helped revolutionize our technology.

124
00:08:41,881 --> 00:08:45,880
Today, copper is all around us.

125
00:08:45,881 --> 00:08:48,880
Everything that you touch during the course of the day

126
00:08:48,881 --> 00:08:52,880
will have copper in it or have some connection to copper.

127
00:08:52,881 --> 00:08:56,640
We have copper in our phones, copper in our computers,

128
00:08:56,641 --> 00:08:59,120
we have copper in our bodies.

129
00:08:59,121 --> 00:09:01,880
Copper is vital to our modern way of life.

130
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:06,120
It's used in electric motors, it's used in copper tubing,

131
00:09:06,121 --> 00:09:07,840
it's used in copper wiring.

132
00:09:07,841 --> 00:09:12,040
Without all of these things, society would not be at the level that it's at.

133
00:09:12,041 --> 00:09:19,120
It's one of the building blocks of making our world as industrialized as it is.

134
00:09:19,121 --> 00:09:22,360
Beyond copper's ability to resist corrosion,

135
00:09:22,361 --> 00:09:30,260
kill bacteria, and conduct both electricity and heat,

136
00:09:30,261 --> 00:09:33,000
it's also beautiful.

137
00:09:33,001 --> 00:09:37,000
In some applications, its veneer is the focal point,

138
00:09:37,001 --> 00:09:41,620
such as in sculptures and roofing.

139
00:09:41,621 --> 00:09:43,820
We're drawn to copper, copper alloys,

140
00:09:43,821 --> 00:09:46,820
because of its soft color, its warmth,

141
00:09:46,821 --> 00:09:50,620
the ability to change its color, change its shape.

142
00:09:50,621 --> 00:09:57,240
It's probably one of the few applications of the product of metals that's exposed and gives you that warmth.

143
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:02,840
In fact, it was copper's distinct red color that first got it noticed.

144
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:09,220
Copper was one of the first metals that was discovered by man nearly 10,000 years ago.

145
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:16,080
And the reason why that is is that copper is one of the few metals that actually exists in the metallic form in nature.

146
00:10:16,081 --> 00:10:22,000
So ancient man might have been walking along and seen some small glint of metal

147
00:10:22,001 --> 00:10:28,120
and been able to pick up and find that they have a piece of a metal that is easy to form.

148
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:33,800
It's soft, and this material would have been easy to form to a variety of shapes.

149
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:38,940
By about 3600 B.C., the demand for copper increased

150
00:10:38,941 --> 00:10:43,440
for use in items as disparate as jewelry and arrowheads.

151
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:47,540
The naturally occurring deposits on the ground became scarcer.

152
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:52,820
But the ancient Egyptians discovered a process called smelting,

153
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:56,640
which separated the metal from the mineral ore in which it was embedded.

154
00:10:57,700 --> 00:11:03,800
The very earliest forms of smelting would simply have been taking the metal

155
00:11:03,801 --> 00:11:07,740
and heating it up over a fire that was sufficiently hot for the metal to melt,

156
00:11:07,741 --> 00:11:11,820
and the smelting would occur naturally by the burn-off of some of the impurities.

157
00:11:11,821 --> 00:11:20,520
By trial and error, the civilizations later realized that you could add certain things to that molten metal,

158
00:11:20,700 --> 00:11:24,320
which would cause the impurities to be removed preferentially.

159
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:29,420
If you add an addition like charcoal, which is basically carbon,

160
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:37,020
then what might happen is that the carbon would combine with the oxygen in some of these impurities,

161
00:11:37,021 --> 00:11:43,500
and that would burn off as carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, leaving the unoxidized metal behind.

162
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:50,740
At about 3000 BC, the ancient Egyptians inadvertently created a completely new metal

163
00:11:50,741 --> 00:11:53,520
when some tin was mixed in with the copper.

164
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:59,860
Copper and tin makes bronze and that's the root of the expression, the Bronze Age,

165
00:11:59,861 --> 00:12:03,900
the period in which people were starting to work with these materials

166
00:12:03,901 --> 00:12:08,480
and to make an ever more complex range of artifacts out of these metals.

167
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:13,440
The development of bronze was a milestone in human history

168
00:12:13,441 --> 00:12:16,480
because pure copper is relatively soft,

169
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:19,480
but bronze is harder and stronger.

170
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:24,080
Farmers could now make a wide array of sturdy tools,

171
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:27,880
and warriors could craft weapons with lethally sharp edges.

172
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:32,080
Without a doubt, the army with the most bronze swords and armor

173
00:12:32,081 --> 00:12:33,880
held a distinct advantage.

174
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:39,340
For many centuries, bronze reigned supreme.

175
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:45,180
Then, from Rome onwards, iron became the basic metal for every Western civilization.

176
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:48,740
But copper would make a spectacular comeback.

177
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:57,000
Copper, mined from vast ore deposits, was destined to connect and energize the world.

178
00:12:57,001 --> 00:13:04,760
About 200,000 pounds of copper are used for each Triton-class nuclear submarine.

179
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:09,780
It is used primarily in the electrical generation and storage system.

180
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:13,600
Copper will return on Modern Marvels.

181
00:13:13,601 --> 00:13:26,360
At Arizona's Phelps Dodge Sierra

182
00:13:26,361 --> 00:13:34,120
Copper Mine, trucks are hard at work hauling ore.

183
00:13:34,121 --> 00:13:36,520
These mining trucks are some of the largest dump trucks

184
00:13:36,521 --> 00:13:38,200
in the world.

185
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:41,720
They can haul as much as 260 tons of ore in a single load.

186
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:48,680
Yet these house-sized haulers couldn't budge a pound of copper without copper.

187
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:52,380
In one of these haul trucks, there's a lot of copper.

188
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:57,120
You'll find it in copper wiring that runs throughout the truck to run the lights.

189
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:59,100
We also use it in the alternators.

190
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:02,320
We also use it in any motors that are on board of the haul trucks.

191
00:14:02,820 --> 00:14:04,580
Of course, there's a lot of copper in the radiator,

192
00:14:04,581 --> 00:14:08,000
which is a vital component to keep the engine cool.

193
00:14:08,001 --> 00:14:16,280
the radiators of the largest mining trucks use 2,500 pounds of copper for tubing and fins

194
00:14:16,281 --> 00:14:25,220
it's here where copper demonstrates another of its unique properties it conveys heat better than

195
00:14:25,221 --> 00:14:31,340
almost any other metal copper is used because it has such a high thermal conductivity the heat in

196
00:14:31,341 --> 00:14:34,840
in the water, which comes from the engine,

197
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,780
is transmitted to the copper,

198
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,460
which sets up lattice vibrations,

199
00:14:41,340 --> 00:14:45,760
which move through from the water side of the copper radiator

200
00:14:45,761 --> 00:14:48,460
to the air side, and it's transmitted out.

201
00:14:50,860 --> 00:14:53,340
Phelps Dodge runs a total of five mines

202
00:14:53,341 --> 00:14:54,980
in the southwestern United States.

203
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:59,980
Together, they produce more than 2 billion pounds of copper annually.

204
00:15:01,340 --> 00:15:06,580
and account for about 60% of total U.S. copper production.

205
00:15:06,581 --> 00:15:10,740
Their mining and refining process has many steps.

206
00:15:10,741 --> 00:15:13,580
The mining process begins with the discovery of an ore body

207
00:15:13,581 --> 00:15:16,340
through exploration drill holes.

208
00:15:16,341 --> 00:15:19,840
We determine where we need to go to extract the ore.

209
00:15:19,841 --> 00:15:25,840
We use large blast hole drills to drill 65 foot deep holes.

210
00:15:25,841 --> 00:15:28,100
After the blast holes are completed,

211
00:15:28,101 --> 00:15:31,040
the explosives crew gets busy.

212
00:15:31,041 --> 00:15:34,860
Laying charges is both a high-tech science and a choreographed art.

213
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:39,400
At the end of this electronic detonator, there is a computer chip.

214
00:15:39,820 --> 00:15:44,520
You are actually programming the firing time into the detonator.

215
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,360
They need to fire in sequential time.

216
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:00,240
181 to all units. This is a one-minute blast warning.

217
00:16:01,040 --> 00:16:03,040
firing the hole for one shot.

218
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:13,040
After the ore has been loosened, the shovels can dig in.

219
00:16:13,041 --> 00:16:17,040
Each one of the buckets of material in that shovel

220
00:16:17,041 --> 00:16:20,040
weighs approximately 65 tons.

221
00:16:20,041 --> 00:16:24,040
So in about four scoops, that shovel can load one of our

222
00:16:24,041 --> 00:16:26,040
260-ton haul trucks.

223
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:30,040
If the ore is mined closer to the surface,

224
00:16:30,041 --> 00:16:35,040
the copper within the ore has been exposed to oxygen.

225
00:16:35,041 --> 00:16:39,040
Oxidation converts the copper to copper oxide.

226
00:16:39,041 --> 00:16:43,040
The copper oxide atoms are not bound as tightly to the ore as copper,

227
00:16:43,041 --> 00:16:46,040
so they can be leached out of the ore.

228
00:16:46,041 --> 00:16:50,040
What we're doing behind me is sprinkling a weak acidic solution

229
00:16:50,041 --> 00:16:53,040
onto some material that contains copper oxide.

230
00:16:53,041 --> 00:16:56,040
And the solution contains sulfuric acid.

231
00:16:56,041 --> 00:17:03,040
This comes in contact with our ore and it converts it into a solution of copper sulfate.

232
00:17:03,041 --> 00:17:11,040
The copper sulfate then travels down to one of our processing facilities where we ultimately extract the copper.

233
00:17:11,041 --> 00:17:16,040
Ore that's not a candidate for leaching takes a different processing path.

234
00:17:16,041 --> 00:17:25,040
The rock that's deeper down in the ore body is a sulfide and it is not leachable by acid, so we have to separate it by flotation.

235
00:17:25,041 --> 00:17:32,040
The first step of the flotation process is to get the rock down to a manageable size.

236
00:17:32,041 --> 00:17:38,540
A gyratory crusher is a large cone that fits inside of a cone-shaped cylinder, and it literally

237
00:17:38,541 --> 00:17:45,540
wobbles around and when a rock gets in between the walls of the mantle and the liner, it

238
00:17:45,541 --> 00:17:49,040
crushes it by wobbling around.

239
00:17:49,041 --> 00:17:54,640
Before the ore can be refined, it must be ground down to the consistency of sand in

240
00:17:54,641 --> 00:18:03,280
massive drums called ball mills. Each 16-foot high, 19-foot long mill is filled

241
00:18:03,281 --> 00:18:08,700
with softball-sized steel balls that pulverize the ore.

242
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:14,320
At the same time, water and chemical agents are added to the mix, creating

243
00:18:14,321 --> 00:18:16,640
slurry.

244
00:18:17,260 --> 00:18:22,060
We then put it through a machine where we agitate the slurry with bubbles and

245
00:18:22,061 --> 00:18:28,060
and the copper mineral sticks to the bubble, floats to the top, we scoop it off, and then

246
00:18:28,061 --> 00:18:30,727
filter off the water and send it to the smelter.

247
00:18:30,728 --> 00:18:33,060
At the smelter, the concentrate is heated,

248
00:18:33,061 --> 00:18:39,060
it's melted, and the copper material separates further.

249
00:18:39,061 --> 00:18:49,060
The metal leaving the smelter is 99.6% copper, but not pure enough for most electrical applications.

250
00:18:49,061 --> 00:18:52,980
This impure copper is cast into slabs called anodes.

251
00:18:55,200 --> 00:19:00,020
The anodes are further purified using a process called electrorefining.

252
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:08,440
In electrorefining, racks of anodes are suspended in an electrified acid solution for 10 days.

253
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:17,400
During this period, the copper dissolves and is attracted to a negatively charged cathode,

254
00:19:17,401 --> 00:19:19,720
leaving any impurities behind.

255
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:25,640
The resulting cathode is 99.99% pure copper.

256
00:19:27,880 --> 00:19:30,840
A similar process called electrowinning

257
00:19:30,841 --> 00:19:34,320
is used to extract copper from liquid copper sulfate

258
00:19:34,321 --> 00:19:36,400
that comes from the leaching process.

259
00:19:38,080 --> 00:19:42,040
In the electrowinning process, we use electricity

260
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:45,760
that charges the ions in the solution

261
00:19:45,761 --> 00:19:49,540
and that causes the copper to plate out as a solid.

262
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:52,440
If you could see inside the electrowinning cell,

263
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:57,100
you would see little electrons and protons on the copper.

264
00:19:57,240 --> 00:20:00,860
You'd see the copper moving towards the starter sheet

265
00:20:00,861 --> 00:20:05,180
and actually plating it, actually attaching and becoming a solid.

266
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:07,900
Over a period of five to seven days,

267
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:11,180
the copper gets thicker on the starter sheet

268
00:20:11,181 --> 00:20:14,800
until it's heavy enough for us to actually pull out

269
00:20:14,801 --> 00:20:16,320
and remove the copper.

270
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:23,400
These 200-pound cathodes are also 99.99% copper.

271
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:31,560
The mining and refining operations run 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.

272
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:36,120
Like most copper mines, the ore that's mined from Sierraita

273
00:20:36,121 --> 00:20:38,480
has a low percentage of copper in it.

274
00:20:40,440 --> 00:20:43,440
Each ton of ore yields just six pounds of copper.

275
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:51,800
But many copper products don't rely on a supply of virgin metal from the mine.

276
00:20:51,801 --> 00:20:56,800
About 1.5 million tons of scrap copper are recycled every year.

277
00:20:56,801 --> 00:21:00,800
Copper is one of the metals that is 100% recyclable.

278
00:21:00,801 --> 00:21:06,800
Most of the copper and copper alloys that you have around you today are recycled from previous copper applications.

279
00:21:06,801 --> 00:21:13,800
You could be using copper tube in your house today that has recycled material from the jewelry that Cleopatra wore years ago.

280
00:21:13,801 --> 00:21:16,140
years ago.

281
00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:18,640
Recycled copper can be used for almost anything

282
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:22,400
except for electrical wire, because refining the metal back

283
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:28,140
to near 100% purity isn't cost effective.

284
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,560
One of the big consumers of recycled copper

285
00:21:30,660 --> 00:21:33,760
is the plumbing industry.

286
00:21:33,860 --> 00:21:36,460
Plumbers frequently choose to install copper pipe

287
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:40,560
because it's naturally corrosion resistant.

288
00:21:40,660 --> 00:21:42,600
It frequently outlasts the building

289
00:21:42,601 --> 00:21:46,600
in which it's installed.

290
00:21:46,601 --> 00:21:50,600
Forming the copper pipe, or tube as it's referred to in the industry,

291
00:21:50,601 --> 00:21:54,600
is a fairly straightforward process.

292
00:21:54,601 --> 00:21:58,600
Everyone picks up a tube of toothpaste in the morning,

293
00:21:58,601 --> 00:22:01,074
squeezes that tube of toothpaste, and it comes out the tube.

294
00:22:01,075 --> 00:22:02,600
Making copper tube is the same thing.

295
00:22:02,601 --> 00:22:06,600
We take a fillet of copper, or a slug of copper,

296
00:22:06,601 --> 00:22:10,600
we put it through an extrusion press, which is the toothpaste tube,

297
00:22:10,601 --> 00:22:15,600
toothpaste tube, we squeeze it under very high pressure and copper tube comes out the end.

298
00:22:15,601 --> 00:22:20,600
But the only difference is that we use a mandrel or some method to make sure that there's

299
00:22:20,601 --> 00:22:25,600
an opening or a hole in the center of the copper tube.

300
00:22:25,601 --> 00:22:30,600
Modern plumbers weren't the first to notice copper's plumbing potential.

301
00:22:30,601 --> 00:22:34,129
The Egyptians produced some of the earliest copper plumbing.

302
00:22:34,130 --> 00:22:35,600
What they probably didn't

303
00:22:35,601 --> 00:22:40,600
realize is that in addition to having a material that they could form into plumbing pipes,

304
00:22:40,601 --> 00:22:47,940
they were actually purifying their water at the same time and that the antimicrobial properties of the copper were killing

305
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:50,340
bacteria and contaminants within the water

306
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:58,040
Today copper's antimicrobial properties have compelled medical experts to study the metals potential

307
00:22:58,500 --> 00:23:05,620
We understand that through research ongoing research that it has the ability to kill certain pathogens in a

308
00:23:05,621 --> 00:23:11,020
situation where it was with copper you would see over a very short period of

309
00:23:11,021 --> 00:23:15,620
time that the copper would not allow the bio load or that product to grow it

310
00:23:15,621 --> 00:23:22,020
would actually bring it to an end in the near future copper may be showing up in

311
00:23:22,021 --> 00:23:27,100
hospitals on doorknobs and handrails to stop the spread of bacteria such as

312
00:23:27,101 --> 00:23:34,840
staphylococcus and E. coli copper isn't just used to build practical items it's

313
00:23:34,841 --> 00:23:38,060
also used to create everything from the biggest bells

314
00:23:38,061 --> 00:23:38,980
to national icons.

315
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:44,920
We're in no danger of running out of copper.

316
00:23:44,921 --> 00:23:49,580
Known resources are estimated at nearly 8.1 trillion pounds,

317
00:23:49,581 --> 00:23:52,780
of which only about 0.75 trillion pounds,

318
00:23:52,781 --> 00:23:56,900
or less than 10% have been mined.

319
00:23:56,901 --> 00:23:59,340
Copper will return on Modern Marvels.

320
00:23:59,341 --> 00:24:05,580
This is the World Peace Bell.

321
00:24:05,581 --> 00:24:12,100
12 feet high and 66,000 pounds, it's the largest swinging bell in the world.

322
00:24:12,101 --> 00:24:20,580
Located in Newport, Kentucky, it was built to commemorate the turn of the millennium.

323
00:24:20,581 --> 00:24:26,580
Like most bells, it's made of a copper alloy, often referred to as bell bronze.

324
00:24:26,581 --> 00:24:31,820
The world's peace bell is made of 80% copper and 20% tin.

325
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:36,260
The reason bells are made of 80% copper and 20% tin

326
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:38,820
is the quality of sound.

327
00:24:38,920 --> 00:24:42,360
Up until around 1600, they experimented

328
00:24:42,460 --> 00:24:46,300
a lot with different copper, zinc, tin,

329
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:49,700
and it was determined that the combination of 80% copper

330
00:24:49,800 --> 00:24:54,900
and 20% tin produced the best sound.

331
00:24:54,901 --> 00:24:59,060
The bell was made the same way bells have been made since the Middle Ages.

332
00:24:59,061 --> 00:25:01,940
Molten bronze was poured into a mold.

333
00:25:01,941 --> 00:25:07,260
But pouring metal for a bell this large presented a daunting new challenge.

334
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:12,140
We had about 80,000 pounds of metal ready to be poured.

335
00:25:12,141 --> 00:25:16,140
We calculated that it had to be done in four minutes and 54 seconds.

336
00:25:16,141 --> 00:25:20,140
If it was too slow, we'd end up like the Liberty Bell in Philadelphia.

337
00:25:20,141 --> 00:25:26,140
If it didn't cool uniformly, the danger would be that the bell would crack the first time it would hit,

338
00:25:26,600 --> 00:25:28,600
because the metal would be inconsistent.

339
00:25:29,340 --> 00:25:31,800
The final cooling stage would be inconsistent.

340
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:36,860
The Bell's designers from Burton Bells in Cincinnati, Ohio,

341
00:25:37,400 --> 00:25:41,000
held their breath the first time the switch was thrown to release the striking hammer.

342
00:25:43,380 --> 00:25:46,020
Of course, we were all worried about what would happen.

343
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:48,660
We hoped we didn't have another Liberty Bell on our hands.

344
00:25:48,661 --> 00:25:54,000
18 seconds before midnight, the switch was turned on, and it struck right on it.

345
00:25:55,900 --> 00:25:58,100
It was the first strike we knew it was good.

346
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:03,540
Burden Bells has been making bells since 1842.

347
00:26:04,960 --> 00:26:08,300
Today it operates the oldest bell foundry in the United States.

348
00:26:10,340 --> 00:26:16,260
It makes about 250 large bells each year, used mostly by churches and universities.

349
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:24,260
It casts bells using a method it developed which uses sand instead of clay to make each mold.

350
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:29,780
Sand is more efficient as it dries and hardens much faster.

351
00:26:31,780 --> 00:26:35,140
The sand is formed into a bell shape using an aluminum pattern.

352
00:26:37,860 --> 00:26:45,380
What we're doing is we're packing the sand nice and tight so that it'll create that perfect

353
00:26:45,381 --> 00:26:48,380
cavity that the bell is sitting in.

354
00:26:48,381 --> 00:26:50,380
The sand will get really hard like concrete.

355
00:26:50,381 --> 00:26:54,380
Then we'll roll this thing back over and you'll see the inside profile of the bell.

356
00:26:54,381 --> 00:27:01,380
That's when we'll put that piece of the flask on and we'll put more sand in and do the exact same thing.

357
00:27:01,381 --> 00:27:05,380
Then when we break the flask apart, we'll pull the pattern out.

358
00:27:05,381 --> 00:27:11,380
That way we can restack the two pieces of flask together and create a hollow cavity

359
00:27:11,381 --> 00:27:17,600
that's the exact replica of the inside of the bell and the exact replica of the outside of the bell.

360
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:26,440
We then melt bronze, bring it up to 2200 degrees, pour it in the sand mold, wait for it to cool,

361
00:27:27,440 --> 00:27:33,500
break it out, and then the arduous task of cleaning it up starts.

362
00:27:33,980 --> 00:27:39,060
As it comes out of the cores, when we break it loose, it's not very good looking.

363
00:27:39,980 --> 00:27:42,560
Verdon also uses bronze to make statues.

364
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:48,700
For centuries, artists have valued bronze for its beauty and durability.

365
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:55,300
Some statues are cast using a process similar to the one used to make bells.

366
00:27:56,700 --> 00:28:00,120
But other statues, including America's most revered of all,

367
00:28:00,680 --> 00:28:03,860
the Statue of Liberty, are made with pure copper.

368
00:28:04,860 --> 00:28:07,900
It looks like it could be a solid piece of copper, but it's not.

369
00:28:07,901 --> 00:28:12,460
Inside is an iron tower that was designed by Eiffel,

370
00:28:13,240 --> 00:28:16,440
and that is what is referred to as an armature.

371
00:28:16,720 --> 00:28:21,800
It's a fairly complex support structure that holds the skin of the Statue of Liberty.

372
00:28:22,620 --> 00:28:28,500
Lady Liberty's skin was sculpted from over 160,000 pounds of pure copper sheet.

373
00:28:30,220 --> 00:28:32,960
Before the skin could be riveted onto the iron armature,

374
00:28:33,820 --> 00:28:36,400
each of the 300 plates were hammered into shape

375
00:28:36,401 --> 00:28:40,180
using an ancient metal smithing technique called repoussé.

376
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:45,200
This process is still used to form intricate details.

377
00:28:46,900 --> 00:28:51,480
Coppersmith Larry Stearns is using it to fashion a rooftop ornament called a finial.

378
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:56,640
Repoussé is a process of hammering sheet into a negative form.

379
00:28:56,641 --> 00:29:02,180
To perform the repoussé technique, you start with a blank of generally soft copper

380
00:29:02,181 --> 00:29:04,580
and start hammering it into that form.

381
00:29:04,581 --> 00:29:10,080
As you hammer it, it becomes harder and harder to the point where it will become brittle and split.

382
00:29:11,020 --> 00:29:15,260
So typically you are also doing a process called annealing.

383
00:29:18,980 --> 00:29:21,420
Annealing is simply heating up the copper.

384
00:29:22,140 --> 00:29:24,620
But the science behind the process isn't so simple.

385
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:30,200
In copper, the atoms are arranged in a particular crystal structure.

386
00:29:30,201 --> 00:29:36,500
And there comes a point where the only way that you can continue to deform the material

387
00:29:36,501 --> 00:29:42,180
is if you allow the rearrangements to occur at the boundaries between the crystals.

388
00:29:42,780 --> 00:29:48,680
And in annealing, you heat the material up to a temperature that's below its melting point,

389
00:29:48,820 --> 00:29:52,580
but high enough for the atoms to be able to rearrange themselves.

390
00:29:53,520 --> 00:29:56,000
And then you can deform the material again.

391
00:29:56,001 --> 00:30:02,180
And so that's why in copper you need to be able to work and anneal and work in a repeated fashion.

392
00:30:03,080 --> 00:30:07,300
I still have a long way to go, but you can see that the shape is starting to develop.

393
00:30:09,860 --> 00:30:12,980
Though copper can be fashioned into ornate shapes quickly,

394
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:20,200
it can take 10 years or more for the signature copper green, called patina, to form.

395
00:30:20,820 --> 00:30:25,980
Patina is actually a crystalline structure that builds onto the surface of the copper.

396
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:33,380
After several months of wet weather, the surface will be a pretty uniform brown,

397
00:30:33,680 --> 00:30:37,900
and it is stable in that color for up to 10 to 15 years,

398
00:30:38,200 --> 00:30:42,660
and then you will start seeing a very slight hint of the green building.

399
00:30:43,260 --> 00:30:47,940
For those who just can't wait, there's a way to accelerate nature's pace.

400
00:30:49,860 --> 00:30:53,500
Coppersmiths can apply ammonium sulfate or other oxidizing chemicals

401
00:30:53,501 --> 00:30:56,100
that speed up the patina process considerably.

402
00:30:57,380 --> 00:30:59,060
Bright copper ages within minutes.

403
00:31:01,820 --> 00:31:05,020
But the stately green color of a weathered copper roof

404
00:31:05,021 --> 00:31:08,260
isn't the only reason homeowners invest in copper roofing.

405
00:31:09,700 --> 00:31:12,500
Properly installed standing seam copper roof

406
00:31:12,501 --> 00:31:15,280
in 16-ounce copper will last 100 years.

407
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:17,580
You can install a roof and not think about it

408
00:31:17,581 --> 00:31:21,140
for another 100 years versus an asphalt shingle,

409
00:31:21,260 --> 00:31:22,600
which might go 15.

410
00:31:23,500 --> 00:31:26,160
But you'd better have a fat wallet.

411
00:31:26,161 --> 00:31:28,740
A copper roof or an average-sized house

412
00:31:28,741 --> 00:31:31,040
could set you back as much as $30,000.

413
00:31:31,041 --> 00:31:34,380
Copper has proven its durability

414
00:31:34,381 --> 00:31:36,680
because we can look at the cathedrals in Europe

415
00:31:36,681 --> 00:31:38,840
from hundreds and hundreds of years ago

416
00:31:38,841 --> 00:31:41,680
where that same copper on the roof exists today.

417
00:31:41,681 --> 00:31:47,300
The chemical process that causes copper roofs

418
00:31:47,301 --> 00:31:50,800
to develop a patina coating is the reason they last so long.

419
00:31:50,801 --> 00:31:56,380
Patina is nothing more than what we would refer to in iron as a rust.

420
00:31:56,880 --> 00:32:03,740
And what it does, it forms a barrier between the atmosphere and the base metal to give us a protective coating.

421
00:32:05,840 --> 00:32:10,340
Roofs and bells showcase how copper is an ideal medium for large projects.

422
00:32:11,160 --> 00:32:13,360
But copper can go small, too.

423
00:32:14,060 --> 00:32:17,180
You'll find it in almost every piece of electronics.

424
00:32:17,181 --> 00:32:23,180
Some asphalt roofing shingles contain copper oxide granules.

425
00:32:23,181 --> 00:32:28,180
The biostatic properties of the copper inhibit the growth of unsightly algae.

