00:35:08,202 --> 00:35:11,720
It would block their fabrication and cause a defect that would kill the circuit.

458
00:35:12,620 --> 00:35:16,640
It wasn't by accident that copper connectors ended up in the newest computers.

459
00:35:18,040 --> 00:35:22,460
Since the power of electricity was first harnessed in the late 1800s,

460
00:35:22,620 --> 00:35:27,340
the leading use for copper has been for the distribution and control of electrical current.

461
00:35:29,640 --> 00:35:32,740
A neutral atom of copper has 29 electrons.

462
00:35:33,480 --> 00:35:38,520
It's a good electrical conductor because a copper atom gives up its outermost electron easily.

463
00:35:39,880 --> 00:35:44,600
This 29th electron moves freely from the vicinity of one copper atom to another.

464
00:35:44,601 --> 00:35:51,200
If one could see inside the structure of an atom, then what you would see is electrons

465
00:35:51,201 --> 00:35:56,920
that were drifting through between the copper atoms, but almost unimpeded.

466
00:35:57,400 --> 00:36:04,940
Whereas in other metals and non-metallic elements, the transfer of electrons has to happen more

467
00:36:04,941 --> 00:36:09,160
specifically by the electrons hopping from one atom to the next.

468
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:13,980
It's very easy to accelerate electrons in the presence of an electric field.

469
00:36:14,600 --> 00:36:21,600
As a result, copper wire is extremely sensitive to electrical pulses.

470
00:36:21,601 --> 00:36:27,600
Only silver conducts electricity better than copper, but copper is much less costly.

471
00:36:27,601 --> 00:36:34,600
Today's world is crisscrossed with a lattice of electrical wires, bringing power to homes and businesses.

472
00:36:34,601 --> 00:36:40,600
Copper is used almost exclusively for all wires, except those spanning long distances.

473
00:36:40,601 --> 00:36:47,600
Aluminum works better for those since it's lighter and keeps overhead lines from sagging.

474
00:36:47,601 --> 00:36:57,600
Serowire in Ogden, Utah, manufactures a variety of wire gauges for use in homes and businesses.

475
00:36:57,601 --> 00:37:05,600
The different sizes are drawn to a precise and consistent diameter through a series of dies, each one smaller than the last.

476
00:37:05,601 --> 00:37:11,420
It's not as simple as stretching it because you can't control the process if all you're

477
00:37:11,421 --> 00:37:16,200
doing is like a taffy pole going from a large size and pulling it down.

478
00:37:16,201 --> 00:37:18,420
You have to control the physical diameter.

479
00:37:18,421 --> 00:37:24,880
When you go to the next die, you're taking another 25% cut and you have a perfectly round,

480
00:37:24,881 --> 00:37:30,720
perfectly sized diameter when you go to the next die.

481
00:37:30,721 --> 00:37:33,960
The CO wraps multiple strands of copper together

482
00:37:34,060 --> 00:37:36,760
to create the cables that will be used to transmit larger

483
00:37:36,860 --> 00:37:40,960
electrical currents in commercial buildings.

484
00:37:41,060 --> 00:37:45,560
Because the commercial products carry so much higher voltage

485
00:37:45,660 --> 00:37:49,580
and amperage, they're composed of stranded conductors

486
00:37:49,680 --> 00:37:51,180
so that we maintain flexibility.

487
00:37:54,580 --> 00:37:57,880
Copper cables deliver more than electrical power.

488
00:37:57,881 --> 00:38:01,340
deliver communications.

489
00:38:01,341 --> 00:38:04,640
In 1866, the first transatlantic cable

490
00:38:04,641 --> 00:38:08,680
succeeded in carrying a message 2,500 miles

491
00:38:08,681 --> 00:38:11,040
from New York to London.

492
00:38:11,041 --> 00:38:12,720
The core of the transatlantic cable

493
00:38:12,721 --> 00:38:18,640
consisted of seven strands of the best quality copper wire.

494
00:38:18,641 --> 00:38:21,240
Although silica glass fibers have supplanted copper

495
00:38:21,241 --> 00:38:23,580
as the signal-carrying medium, there's

496
00:38:23,581 --> 00:38:25,540
far more copper than glass in the latest

497
00:38:25,541 --> 00:38:32,780
trans-oceanic cables. The cables are sheathed in copper, which carries power

498
00:38:32,781 --> 00:38:39,760
to pumps. The pumps power lasers, which convey the information. The copper also

499
00:38:39,761 --> 00:38:44,200
protects the data by creating an electromagnetic shield that blocks stray

500
00:38:44,201 --> 00:38:52,500
signals. Like other electrical devices, the cables require pure copper, but new

501
00:38:52,501 --> 00:38:57,120
copper alloys are expanding copper's reach beyond the world of electronics.

502
00:39:00,420 --> 00:39:01,909
In 1982, the U.S.

503
00:39:01,910 --> 00:39:08,480
Mint changed the composition of a penny from 95% copper to just 2.6% copper

504
00:39:08,481 --> 00:39:14,760
and 97.4% zinc to keep production costs under one cent per coin.

505
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:21,020
Copper will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

506
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:26,240
The industrial importance of copper in the 21st century

507
00:39:26,241 --> 00:39:29,140
has been extended by the ease with which it combines

508
00:39:29,141 --> 00:39:29,960
with other metals.

509
00:39:32,240 --> 00:39:35,820
Tin and zinc have long been principal alloying elements.

510
00:39:37,240 --> 00:39:41,220
But now there are many others, including aluminum, beryllium,

511
00:39:41,380 --> 00:39:42,940
chromium, and manganese.

512
00:39:43,660 --> 00:39:46,880
They form alloys with unique mechanical and physical properties.

513
00:39:48,720 --> 00:39:51,700
Over 400 copper alloys are in use today.

514
00:39:52,500 --> 00:39:56,500
In the near future, one new copper alloy may be showing up

515
00:39:56,501 --> 00:40:00,500
in a tag sewn into your clothing and attached to almost all of

516
00:40:00,501 --> 00:40:05,500
the products you buy. Engineers are working on a mixture

517
00:40:05,501 --> 00:40:09,500
of copper, nickel, and silicon that will maintain copper's

518
00:40:09,501 --> 00:40:12,500
conductivity, but will also be strong enough to support tiny

519
00:40:12,501 --> 00:40:18,500
antennae only 35 microns thick. That's one-tenth the thickness

520
00:40:18,501 --> 00:40:22,100
one-tenth the thickness of a sheet of paper.

521
00:40:22,101 --> 00:40:28,460
An antenna embedded in a tag will transmit information about you or a product to a computer

522
00:40:28,461 --> 00:40:37,260
to inform the retailer about your consumer preferences or the status of its inventory.

523
00:40:37,261 --> 00:40:42,660
Ansonia Copper and Brass in Waterbury, Connecticut, make specialty alloys for customers with very

524
00:40:42,661 --> 00:40:44,540
specific requirements.

525
00:40:44,541 --> 00:40:51,920
One of those alloys would be a particular alloy we make for the airspace industry where

526
00:40:51,921 --> 00:40:58,300
the customer needed good electrical conductivity, strength within the wire so they could withstand

527
00:40:58,301 --> 00:41:05,220
constant use, and also to go from ground level to 45,000 feet in the air where the material

528
00:41:05,221 --> 00:41:10,660
actually grows because of the change in altitude.

529
00:41:10,661 --> 00:41:15,900
The custom alloy, which will be used to make airplane struts, is composed of 90% copper

530
00:41:15,901 --> 00:41:18,700
and 10% aluminum.

531
00:41:18,701 --> 00:41:24,880
In this application, the aluminum adds hardness to the copper.

532
00:41:24,881 --> 00:41:29,540
The production process is monitored every step of the way, because even a slight variation

533
00:41:29,541 --> 00:41:36,600
in the predetermined formula could result in a catastrophic failure for one of the airplanes.

534
00:41:36,601 --> 00:41:41,100
and during the casting, casting supervisors take samples

535
00:41:41,101 --> 00:41:43,500
to ensure that the mixture is exactly right

536
00:41:43,501 --> 00:41:45,500
and free of impurities.

537
00:41:45,501 --> 00:41:47,800
We take that sample and we pour it off

538
00:41:47,801 --> 00:41:49,940
and we make a slug, and then we send it over

539
00:41:49,941 --> 00:41:54,540
to our analytical laboratory, and we take it through an analysis

540
00:41:54,541 --> 00:41:59,200
process to determine are we on analysis at this time.

541
00:41:59,201 --> 00:42:01,360
If we are, we go ahead and pour.

542
00:42:01,361 --> 00:42:04,000
If we aren't, then we make corrections back

543
00:42:04,001 --> 00:42:05,000
in the melting furnace.

544
00:42:08,460 --> 00:42:12,600
Chemists in the lab quickly assess each sample.

545
00:42:12,601 --> 00:42:15,300
One instrument they use, a spectrometer,

546
00:42:15,301 --> 00:42:17,760
analyzes the atomic composition of each sample.

547
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:24,620
After a laser slices some atoms from the sample,

548
00:42:24,621 --> 00:42:28,600
a light is then filtered through that sliver of atoms.

549
00:42:28,601 --> 00:42:31,240
The light projects a prism of colors.

550
00:42:31,241 --> 00:42:34,340
Each color represents an element.

551
00:42:34,341 --> 00:42:36,440
The amount of each color tells the chemists

552
00:42:36,441 --> 00:42:40,040
how much of each metal is in the sample.

553
00:42:40,041 --> 00:42:45,940
And this sample represents 7,600 pounds of metal

554
00:42:45,941 --> 00:42:47,040
in the casting shop.

555
00:42:47,041 --> 00:42:51,360
We have about 20 minutes to determine 25 elements

556
00:42:51,361 --> 00:42:54,780
and report to the casting shop.

557
00:42:54,781 --> 00:42:57,120
After an alloy is cast and shaped,

558
00:42:57,121 --> 00:42:59,740
Further tests analyze more of its physical properties.

559
00:43:01,060 --> 00:43:03,520
The tensile tester actually pulls the material,

560
00:43:03,860 --> 00:43:08,420
and it's measuring the amount of strength it takes to pull the material until it actually breaks.

561
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:13,440
That defines the ductility of the material.

562
00:43:14,020 --> 00:43:16,640
All the alloys that are produced here have a specification,

563
00:43:17,080 --> 00:43:19,420
and we have to produce the material and make it to that specification.

564
00:43:21,020 --> 00:43:24,180
Just as the ancients who first combined copper and tin

565
00:43:24,181 --> 00:43:26,780
couldn't have predicted where bronze would take them,

566
00:43:27,120 --> 00:43:32,120
21st century metallurgists can only guess where the new alloys

567
00:43:32,121 --> 00:43:36,120
will lead us. It has been around for such a long

568
00:43:36,121 --> 00:43:39,120
time that we have learned what we think are most of the

569
00:43:39,121 --> 00:43:41,120
technologies, but we only think that.

570
00:43:41,121 --> 00:43:45,120
We're sure, beyond a shadow of a doubt, copper will be an

571
00:43:45,121 --> 00:43:49,120
integral, instrumental metal in everything that we do from this

572
00:43:49,121 --> 00:43:52,120
point forward. The same properties that we know

573
00:43:52,121 --> 00:43:56,120
of and use will continue to allow us to use it in the new

574
00:43:56,121 --> 00:43:57,920
in the new technologies in the future.

575
00:44:00,620 --> 00:44:03,920
Count copper among the marvels we seldom celebrate,

576
00:44:04,020 --> 00:44:07,860
but have helped advance the march of our technology.

577
00:44:07,960 --> 00:44:11,100
This largely unsung metal and its alloys

578
00:44:11,200 --> 00:44:13,100
promise to keep pace with our ability

579
00:44:13,200 --> 00:44:16,440
to mine technological treasure from nature.

